Track whether the pointer is over an X11 window by the window that has it,
not by counting enter and leave events: the compositor can't send a leave for
a surface destroyed under the pointer (a menu, a tooltip, a closed chat), so
the count stayed above zero for good.  From then on, while the pointer was
over Wayland windows, the X pointer stayed over whichever X11 window was at
its last position, and mouse buttons the compositor forwarded for push to
talk (cosmic-comp's xwayland_eavesdropping.pointer) went to that window --
Steam's, say -- instead of the root window, where Discord listens.

--- a/hw/xwayland/xwayland-input.c
+++ b/hw/xwayland/xwayland-input.c
@@ -3211,7 +3211,16 @@
     if (!xwl_seat)
         return FALSE;
 
-    pointer_crossing = (xwl_seat->pointer_enter_count > 0);
+    /* Whether the pointer is over one of our windows.  This used to be
+     * pointer_enter_count > 0, but that count never goes down for a window
+     * destroyed under the pointer -- the compositor can't send a leave for
+     * a dead surface -- and once it was stuck, the sprite stayed over
+     * whatever X11 window was at the pointer's last position while the
+     * pointer was over Wayland windows: buttons forwarded by compositors
+     * that let X11 programs see input meant for others (push to talk) went
+     * to that window instead of the root window.
+     */
+    pointer_crossing = (xwl_seat->focus_window != NULL);
     master = GetMaster(device, POINTER_OR_FLOAT);
     if (!master || !master->lastSlave)
         return FALSE;
